<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n42867" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">scansĭlis</span></b> , e, adj.
		scando, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>that may be climbed.</i>
		</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ficus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					17, 11, 16, § 84</span></i>
			: gradus superiores scansiles, Schol. <i><span class="en">Juv. 7,
			45</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Amm. 16, 10,
			14</span></i>.—* </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>: lex annorum, i. e. <i>the law of critical years</i>
			 (placed at certain distances or steps from each
			other) or <i>climacterics</i>, <i><span class="en">Plin. 7, 49,
				50, § 161</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
